    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey, i'm working on some year-end stuff for my company and i'm a bit stuck. can you walk me through the straight-line depreciation calculation for a piece of equipment? it cost us $45,000, it's got an estimated salvage value of $5,000, and we expect it to last for 8 years. i just want to make sure i'm doing the steps right for the documentation.",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To calculate straight-line depreciation, you'll need to divide the cost of the asset by its useful life in years. In your case, the total depreciation per year is: Depreciable Cost = Cost - Salvage Value = $45,000 - $5,000 = $40,000. Then, divide by Useful Life (number of years): $40,000 / 8 years = $5,000 per year. This means you'll depreciate $5,000 each year for 8 years.",
      "source": "target",
